P=Path('research/full-proof-integration-20260930/iclr2024-sparse/content.json'); src=json.loads(P.read_text()); E={}
def put(k,title,overview,steps=(),assumptions=None):E['iclr2024-sparse-'+k]=dict(title=title,overview=overview,steps=list(steps),assumptions=assumptions)
put('definitions','Masks, centered outputs, and Harsanyi interactions','The paper uses u=g₀. Its empty interaction is zero. The paper’s input baseline b is the canonical vector r, not the scalar output baseline b.')
put('desiderata','Sparsity, universal matching, and sample-wise transfer','These are evaluation criteria with externally cited empirical evidence, rather than three locally proved theorems.')
put('reconstruction','Exact centered reconstruction of every mask','Expand the centered Möbius coefficients, exchange finite sums, cancel all proper-subset terms, and restore the output baseline.',[
r'''Define $I(T)=\sum_{L\subseteq T}(-1)^{|T|-|L|}g_0(L)$. For every $S\subseteq N$, the claim is $v(x_S)=b+\sum_{T\subseteq S}I(T)$.''',
r'''All subset sums include the empty set, with $I(\varnothing)=g_0(\varnothing)=0$. For fixed $L\subseteq S$, the supersets $T$ in the double sum are bijectively $T=L\cup U$ for $U\subseteq S\setminus L$, and $|T|-|L|=|U|$. Thus $\sum_{T\subseteq S}I(T)=\sum_{L\subseteq S}g_0(L)\sum_{U\subseteq S\setminus L}(-1)^{|U|}=\sum_{L\subseteq S}g_0(L)(1-1)^{|S\setminus L|}=g_0(S)$.''',
r'''Only $L=S$ survives, with inner sum 1; a proper subset has a nonempty difference and inner sum zero. Add b. At $S=\varnothing$, the only interaction is zero and the output is b. For two variables, $I(\{1,2\})=g(\{1,2\})-g(\{1\})-g(\{2\})+b$, and the three nonempty interactions sum to $g(\{1,2\})-b$. The complete shared proof is `proof-finite-mobius-reconstruction-v2`. The Lean proof uses finite insertion induction, separately identified from the author’s exchanged-sum argument.'''])
put('salient-approximation','Approximation using salient interactions','The approximation symbol has no uniform source tolerance. The exact omitted-interaction remainder identifies what is required for numerical approximation.',[r'''For a retained family $\Omega\subseteq2^N$, exact reconstruction gives
$v(x_S)-[b+\sum_{T\subseteq S,T\in\Omega}I(T)]=\sum_{T\subseteq S,T\notin\Omega}I(T)$.
Partition the finite family into retained and omitted terms. The approximation has numerical meaning only if this total remainder is sufficiently small. Individually small omitted terms do not automatically give a certified small error for all masks. This explains the author’s approximation and does not present a newly specified tolerance as a source theorem.'''])
put('taylor-expansion','Infinite multivariate Taylor expansion at the input baseline','This is not an identity for every smooth function. The convergence and representation issue is shared with Lemma 1.')
put('assumption-1alpha','Vanishing interactions above order M','This is a premise of the sparsity argument, not an unconditional conclusion.')
put('assumption-1beta','Global vanishing of derivatives above order M','The quantifiers cover all expansion points and multiindices. The paper also mentions ReLU finite differences, without proving the corresponding implication.')
put('lemma1','Taylor interaction formula grouped by variable support','The author groups a valid Taylor representation by multiindex support, then uses uniqueness over every mask. A general smooth-function reading has an independent validity defect.',[r'''For nonempty S the author claims
$I(S)=\sum_{\kappa\in Q_S}D^\kappa v(r)\prod_{i\in S}(x_i-r_i)^{\kappa_i}/\kappa!$,
where $Q_S$ consists of multiindices positive on S and zero elsewhere. Define this right-hand side as $\widetilde I(S)$ and set $\widetilde I(\varnothing)=0$. Group every masked Taylor expansion by its nonzero-coordinate support to obtain $v(x_S)=b+\sum_{T\subseteq S}\widetilde I(T)$, then invoke coefficient uniqueness for all masks, including the empty mask. A single full-input equality cannot establish uniqueness.
A smooth function need not equal its Taylor series: $v(t)=e^{-1/t^2}$ for nonzero t and $v(0)=0$ has all derivatives zero at 0, but $I(\{1\})=e^{-1}$. This refutes the unrestricted smooth-function reading; adding analyticity would change the original proposition. If Eq. (2) is instead a given valid Taylor representation, that example does not satisfy it and does not refute the conditional grouping argument. It also does not satisfy the global high-order zero-derivative Assumption 1-β, so it does not refute the next finite-order implication. The original formula and proof are retained.'''],['The original lemma does not separately require convergence or equality of its Taylor series. Appendix B.2 uses it before the 1-β⇒1-α implication; this scope gap is separately recorded.'])
put('derivative-cutoff','Derivative cutoff implies interaction cutoff','Globally zero classical mixed partials give zero rectangular differences. The actual coordinate-mask alternating sum is that difference; nonempty centering then recovers the original interaction.',[
r'''Fix $N=\{1,\ldots,n\}$, $v:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$, x, and r. A mask keeps x_i on A and r_i elsewhere. Set $g(A)=v(x_A)$, $b=g(\varnothing)$, and $g_0=g-b$. The source interaction is $I_{g_0}$, so its empty value is zero. Let e_i be a coordinate unit vector and $a_i=x_i-r_i$.
The classical partial is $D_i f(z)=\left.\frac d{dt}f(z+t e_i)\right|_{t=0}$. Encode a multiindex by a nondecreasing coordinate list $(i_1,\ldots,i_q)$, and define $P_0=v$, $P_j=D_{i_j}P_{j-1}$. Classical existence of the stated mixed partials includes actual differentiability of each required prefix along the next coordinate, and 1-β gives $P_q\equiv0$. These existence conditions and the zero value are encoded together; only lists q>M are used. No extra global smoothness of all low derivatives is assumed. C1 alone does not imply existence of high derivatives. A fixed ascending order is used; a reverse convention can use the same difference argument in reverse order, without interchanging mixed partials.''',
r'''Define $\delta_{a,i}f(z)=f(z+a e_i)-f(z)$. If f is differentiable along coordinate i everywhere, its finitely many translated sums and differences are too. Translation gives $D_i(f(\cdot+h))(z)=D_i f(z+h)$ because $(z+t e_i)+h=(z+h)+t e_i$. The one-variable difference rule yields $D_i(\delta_{a,j}f)=\delta_{a,j}(D_i f)$. Induction extends this to arbitrary finite rectangular differences. Only translations and finite additions are used, with no infinite series or higher directional chain rule.''',
r'''Induct on the direction-list length q, allowing arbitrary directions and real steps. For the empty list, both the difference and the order-zero partial are f, and its assumed zero value proves the claim. For a nonempty list, let R denote the remaining differences. The prefix-existence premise applies the induction hypothesis to $D_{i_1}f$, giving $R(D_{i_1}f)=0$. Commutation then gives $D_{i_1}(Rf)=0$. For fixed z, $H(t)=(Rf)(z+t e_{i_1})$ is differentiable on the whole real line with zero derivative. The mean value theorem gives $H(a_{i_1})=H(0)$, so the full rectangular difference is zero. Negative and zero steps require no new restrictions.''',
r'''List the distinct coordinates of S, take steps $a_i=x_i-r_i$, and start at r. Induct on that list. The empty difference is $v(r)=g(\varnothing)$. Adding a previously absent coordinate splits the rectangle into its translated and untranslated parts. On every remaining subset U, the translated vector is exactly the mask for $U\cup\{i\}$ and the untranslated vector is the mask for U. Subtracting the alternating sums is the insertion identity for $I_g(S)$. This bridge holds for an arbitrary model v; no polynomial representation is assumed.''',
r'''If $|S|\ge M+1$, list S in ascending order. The selected multiindex is 1 on S and 0 elsewhere, with total degree |S|>M. The original global 1-β supplies its classical derivative existence and zero value. The analytic lemma makes its rectangular difference zero, and the mask bridge gives $I_g(S)=0$. The step sizes only affect the difference, not derivative existence or its zero premise, so x and r are arbitrary.''',
r'''Since |S|>M≥0, S is nonempty. Subtracting the constant baseline changes only the empty interaction, hence $I_{g_0}(S)=I_g(S)=0$, proving 1-α. Appendix B.2 uses Lemma 1’s infinite Taylor argument; this repair uses the original global 1-β in a finite-difference argument. The separate validity issue of a general Taylor-formula reading remains separate. A given valid Taylor representation gives another conditional grouping argument, whose proposition is not modified here.''',
r'''For $v(z_1,z_2)=5+2z_1-3z_2$ and M=1, all classical mixed partials of total order at least 2 vanish. For arbitrary x,r, the centered singleton interactions are $2(x_1-r_1)$ and $-3(x_2-r_2)$, and the pair interaction is zero. The output baseline $5+2r_1-3r_2$ need not vanish; its centered empty interaction is zero and the cutoff is unchanged.'''],[
'The original C1-function setting is retained. Global high-order mixed partials in 1-β are interpreted as classical existing derivatives; required prefix differentiability unfolds their definition and is not replaced by a total Lean deriv function returning zero.',
'All masks use the same fixed input baseline r and M∈ℕ. No analyticity, infinite Taylor representation, or polynomial premise is added.'])
put('assumption2','Monotonicity of the mean output by mask size','Monotonicity of layer averages is distinct from pointwise monotonicity of every nested mask pair.')
put('assumption3','Polynomial lower bound for mean outputs','p>0. The m=0 quotient is undefined 0/0; the main argument uses m≥1. This boundary is recorded without changing quantifiers.')
put('order-statistics','Order totals, cancellation ratios, and salient counts','η divides by the absolute interaction strength. Zero total strength and zero η require separate treatment in any division argument.')
put('lemma2','Combinatorial formula for mean masked outputs','Reconstruct each mask, count its containing supersets by a bijection, normalize by positive binomial coefficients, and apply the original cutoff.',[
r'''Let $A^{(k)}=\sum_{|T|=k}I(T)$ and n=|N|. Under 1-α, for M≤m≤n the claim is
$\bar g_0^{(m)}=\binom nm^{-1}\sum_{|S|=m}g_0(S)=\sum_{k=1}^M[\binom mk/\binom nk]A^{(k)}$.''',
r'''Use reconstruction on every m-element mask. The empty interaction is zero. Group the remaining terms by order to get $\sum_{|S|=m}g_0(S)=\sum_{k=1}^m\sum_{|S|=m}\sum_{T\subseteq S,|T|=k}I(T)$.''',
r'''For fixed k-element T, m-element supersets S correspond bijectively to (m-k)-element subsets U of N\T via S=T∪U. Therefore I(T) occurs $\binom{n-k}{m-k}$ times. Exchanging finite sums gives $\sum_{|S|=m}\sum_{T\subseteq S,|T|=k}I(T)=\binom{n-k}{m-k}A^{(k)}$.''',
r'''For k≤m≤n, both binomial denominators are positive. The identity $\binom nm\binom mk=\binom nk\binom{n-k}{m-k}$ gives $\binom{n-k}{m-k}/\binom nm=\binom mk/\binom nk$.''',
r'''Divide by $\binom nm$ to obtain the formula through order m. The original cutoff makes each k>M interaction and its order total zero, leaving the sum through M. For m<M the same count works with $\binom mk=0$ when k>m; this supplies the range needed by Theorem 2 without changing this lemma’s stated quantifiers. At m=0 the centered mean is zero.''',
r'''For n=3,M=2 the layer means are $A^{(1)}/3$, $2A^{(1)}/3+A^{(2)}/3$, and $A^{(1)}+A^{(2)}$. These are checks of the general claim. `Harsanyi.Sparsity.mean_centered_cutoff` proves the superset bijection, finite reindexing, binomial ratio, and centered cutoff.'''],['Finite universe with n=|N|','The original centered convention I(empty)=0','Assumption 1-α','M≤m≤n'])
put('lemma3','Full column rank of the consecutive-binomial matrix','Repair the determinant sign; an independent finite-difference induction proves the full kernel statement.',[
r'''For 1≤M<n, if $\sum_{k=1}^M[\binom{n-j}k/\binom nk]w_k=0$ for every j=0,…,M, then every w_k vanishes. The author’s determinant is nonzero, but Eqs. (35)–(36) omit its sign. In the displayed decreasing row order the correct value is $(-1)^{M(M-1)/2}/\prod_{k=1}^M\binom nk$.''',
r'''Expand the first column in Eq. (32). Its only nonzero entry is in the last row, giving cofactor sign $(-1)^{M+1}$. Repeating in one lower dimension gives the next factor $(-1)^M$, continuing to order 1 and producing the sign above. Every binomial denominator is positive, so the corrected determinant still establishes rank.''',
r'''Lean uses finite differences instead of a determinant. Prove: if M≤n and $\sum_{k=0}^M\binom{n-j}k a_k=0$ for j=0,…,M, all a_k vanish. Induct on M. At M=0 the equation is a₀=0. Subtract adjacent rows and use $\binom{n-j}{k+1}-\binom{n-j-1}{k+1}=\binom{n-j-1}k$. This gives the degree-M−1 system for shifted coefficients a_{k+1} and n−1. The induction hypothesis quantifies over arbitrary coefficient functions and n, so it yields a₁,…,a_M=0. Substitute into the first row to obtain a₀=0. Finally take a₀=0 and a_k=w_k/choose(n,k); positive denominators recover w_k=0. `Harsanyi.Sparsity.binomial_matrix_kernel` proves the general statement, not a few fixed matrices.'''],['Positive integers n and M with M<n','All M+1 row equations'])
put('theorem2','Base-n representation of order totals and the δ bound','Construct witnesses without assuming a nonzero first mean. Set every low-order digit to zero, preserving the original existence equations and bounds.',[
r'''Keep the source coefficient equality, |λ^(k)|≤1, digit bounds, witness m₀, and the positive/negative-λ δ bounds. The ellipsis for 0<p<1 is separately recorded. The construction sets every low-order coefficient to zero, avoiding the author’s digit algorithm, G, and k*.''',
r'''Write μ_m for the centered layer mean. Original monotonicity gives 0≤μ₁≤μ_n. Assumption 3 at m′=1 gives μ_n≤n^p μ₁. Split according to whether μ₁ is positive or zero.''',
r'''When μ₁>0 set $C=1+\sum_{k=1}^M|A^{(k)}/\mu_1|>0$, δ=log_n C−p, $\lambda^{(k)}=A^{(k)}/(\mu_1 C)$, and all digits zero. In the original logarithm/binomial domain n>1 and 1≤M≤n, including M=n, one has n^{p+δ}=C, |λ^(k)|≤1, and $A^{(k)}=\lambda^{(k)}n^{p+\delta}\mu_1$. Take m₀=n, so the binomial ratios are 1. Cutoff reconstruction gives $\lambda=\sum_k\lambda^{(k)}=\mu_n/(\mu_1 C)>0$ and all aggregated digits zero. Hence λC=μ_n/μ₁≤n^p. Monotonicity of log to base n>1 gives δ≤log_n(1/λ), the original positive branch. This witness has positive λ, so the negative conditional branch is not triggered.''',
r'''If μ₁=0, the original assumptions give 0≤μ_m≤m^p μ₁=0 for each 1≤m≤n. Thus every layer mean is zero. Recursively at m=k, the counting formula has all lower totals already zero and every k′>k binomial coefficient zero, leaving $0=A^{(k)}/\binom nk$. Positive denominators give A^(k)=0, starting at k=1. Zero means are not incorrectly taken to imply zero output at every mask.''',
r'''Choose m₀=n, λ^(1)=1, all other λ^(k)=0, δ=−p, and every low digit zero. The coefficient equalities have both sides zero; λ=1 is nonzero. The δ bound becomes −p≤0=log_n 1, true because p>0. This handles the author’s zero division, empty G, and low-order witness gaps without adding assumptions.''',
r'''Represent low digits as the finite index family J={i∈ℕ:i<floor(p)}. It is empty when p<1. If the printed constant digit is retained separately, a₀^(k)=0 also satisfies it. The witness works for any finite digit family because every digit is zero. No p≥1 restriction is added and no existence equality is changed. `sparse_original_coefficient_witness` proves the full witness; `FullSparse.theorem2` supplies the actual masked-model adapter. The parameter domain and M=0 gap remain disclosed.'''],['Assumptions 1-α, 2, and 3','p>0; the original logarithm/binomial domain n>1 and M≤n','The original M=0 gap is separately listed; the positive-order construction includes M=n and does not inherit Lemma 3’s M<n restriction','The all-zero low-digit witness includes empty finite index families'])
put('asymptotic-claim','An infinite counterfamily to the Case 2 same-order sparsity inference','The original assumptions permit smooth quadratic models with polynomially small cancellation and every pair interaction salient. The exact Theorems 2–3 remain valid.',[
r'''The exact count is described asymptotically as O(n^{p+δ}/|τη|), usually far below all possible interactions. Small p, moderate cancellation, and suitable thresholds have empirical support, but n-dependent parameters and δ need not give a uniform asymptotic bound. Keep the “most cases” discussion separate. Page 8 Case 2 also claims that R^(k) is much less than choose(n,k) if |η^(k)| is not exponentially small, without a large-k premise. The following infinite family refutes that additional inference alone.''',
r'''For n≥3 with n≡2 or 3 mod 4, let q=choose(n,2)=n(n−1)/2. It is odd: at n=4j+2 it is (2j+1)(4j+1), and at n=4j+3 it is (4j+3)(2j+1). In particular n=4j+3 is infinite. Partition the q pairs into a positive family P of size (q−1)/2 and a negative family Q of size (q+1)/2. Such finite subsets exist. Set c_A=1 on P and −1 on Q, so the pair coefficients sum to −1.''',
r'''Use x=(1,…,1), r=0, and the smooth quadratic model $v_n(z)=\sum_i z_i+\sum_{|A|=2}c_A\prod_{i\in A}z_i$. Its output baseline is zero. Pure-AND coefficient distribution and linearity give singleton interactions 1, pair interactions c_A, and all other interactions zero. Thus A^(1)=n and A^(2)=−1. A finite square-activation network realizes the model via $z_i z_j=((z_i+z_j)^2-z_i^2-z_j^2)/2$. No extra “well-trained” predicate is assumed.''',
r'''Take M=2<n. All global mixed derivatives of total order at least 3 vanish, proving even 1-β; the displayed coefficients give 1-α directly. A fixed pair is contained in a uniform m-element mask with probability choose(m,2)/q, so μ_m=m−choose(m,2)/q for 0≤m≤n. Its increment is $1-m/q\ge1-2/n>0$, proving Assumption 2. For m≥1, μ_m/m=1−(m−1)/(2q) is nonincreasing, so μ_{m′}≥(m′/m)μ_m for 1≤m′≤m. At m′=0 both sides are zero. Assumption 3 holds with the fixed p=1.''',
r'''Choose τ=1/100. Every pair interaction has absolute value 1, so R^(2)=q=choose(n,2), total absolute pair strength q, and η^(2)=−1/q. On the four submasks of a pair, outputs are 0,1,1,2+c_A, all nonnegative, with mean absolute output 3/4 or 5/4. The fixed threshold is small relative to both, matching the source’s qualitative threshold choice. The cancellation magnitude 2/[n(n−1)] decays polynomially, and n|η|→0 places the example in the nearly canceling Case 2. Nevertheless R^(2)/choose(n,2)=1 always.''',
r'''The exact Theorem 3 bound is |A^(2)|/(τ|η^(2)|)=100q, and q≤100q. Thus the exact coefficient-existence and threshold-count theorems remain valid. Only the extra inference from nonexponential cancellation to same-order sparsity fails. The adjacent empirical “most cases” wording is not made universal. There are only n+q=O(n²) nonzero interactions, still far fewer than 2^n, so the example does not refute sparsity relative to all 2^n subsets. This is a project counterexample proof with no new Lean-success claim.'''])
put('theorem3','Upper bound on salient interactions of a fixed order','Threshold counting and the cancellation ratio give the bound; substitute the same coefficient witnesses from Theorem 2 within the original noncanceling Case 1.',[
r'''The source theorem occurs in Case 1, where |η^(k)|≫1/n excludes complete cancellation. Put $B_k=\sum_{|S|=k}|I(S)|$, η^(k)=A^(k)/B_k, τ>0, and R^(k) equal to the number of |I(S)|≥τ terms. In this original range B_k>0 and η^(k)≠0, and $\tau R^{(k)}\le\sum_{|S|=k,|I(S)|\ge\tau}|I(S)|\le B_k=|A^{(k)}|/|\eta^{(k)}|$.''',
r'''Use the threshold on each salient term. The next inequality uses nonnegative absolute values. Finally A^(k)=η^(k)B_k and B_k>0 give the absolute-value quotient. Divide by the positive τ to obtain R^(k)≤|A^(k)|/(τ|η^(k)|).''',
r'''Substitute the same Theorem 2 coefficient representation. Original monotonicity gives μ₁≥0, hence
$R^{(k)}\le\mu_1|\lambda^{(k)}n^{p+\delta}+\sum_i a_i^{(k)}n^i|/(\tau|\eta^{(k)}|)$,
which is source Eq. (6)/(54), with the same low-digit indices. At η=0 division is invalid. At an empty order B_k=0 the salient count is zero, but its ratio 0/0 remains undefined; neither boundary is silently added to Case 1. `salient_count_bound` proves threshold/cancellation, `salient_bound_original_coefficients` proves substitution, `sparse_original_T2_T3` constructs witnesses from the original three conditions rather than adding them as assumptions, and `FullSparse.theorem3` is the actual model adapter. The report distinguishes these scopes.'''],['The original noncanceling Case 1, with η≠0','τ>0','The same A^(k) coefficient representation as in Theorem 2'])
put('axiom-linearity','Linearity of interactions','The sum of two centered games is centered. Substitute the original u,u₁,u₂ into the general finite linearity identity; the empty case is included.')
put('axiom-dummy','Dummy-variable property','The source A.1(3) already requires S nonempty and i∉S. Take the shared constant c=u({i}) and restrict the original all-T additive premise to U⊆S. The complete nonempty-dummy proof then applies. The CVPR empty-S version cannot be substituted.',assumptions=['g₀(empty)=0','S is explicitly nonempty, as in source A.1(3)'])
put('axiom-symmetry','Symmetry of interactions','The original equality for all T⊆N\{i,j} implies the equality on each U⊆S. Both i and j are absent from S, so apply the shared symmetry proof to the source u.')
put('axiom-anonymity','Anonymity under relabeling','Treat the source permutation π as an equivalence of finite variables. Relabel masks, u, and interactions. The general shared relabeling proof applies, retaining the centered empty convention.')
put('axiom-recursive','Recursive contextual-difference identity','The source has i∉S. The shared context-difference identity gives I(S∪{i})=I_{u(·∪{i})}(S)−I_u(S). Expanding the first interaction recovers the source recursion.')
put('axiom-distribution','Coefficient distribution of a pure interaction','The displayed u_T(S)=c when T⊆S, and zero otherwise, is the shared unanimity game. Its Möbius coefficient is c only at S=T. At T=empty it is the constant game c and its empty coefficient is c; it cannot simultaneously be treated as the globally centered u.',assumptions=['Use the displayed u_T definition. At T=empty it does not satisfy g₀(empty)=0, although the Möbius identity remains meaningful.'])
put('lemma4','Interaction expansion of higher marginal benefits','Set g=u=g₀ in the shared higherMarginal identity. The source T⊆N\S gives disjoint T,S. The shared proof does not require nonemptiness, so empty T and empty S retain the exact equality.',assumptions=['T⊆N\S, equivalently T and S are disjoint'])
put('theorem4','Shapley values as equal interaction shares','Set g=u, the same finite N, and i∈N in factorialShapley. The source weight 1/[n choose(n−1,|S|)] equals |S|!(n−|S|−1)!/n!. The complete finite convolution includes L=empty and gives each coefficient weight 1/(|S|+1). The original Beta/zero-parameter/free-index errors stay visible in source and issues; the repaired proof preserves the original indicator.',assumptions=['Finite universe, i∈N, and the classical Shapley definition'])
put('theorem5','Interaction expansion of the Shapley interaction index','The source SII weight |S|!(n−|T|−|S|)!/(n−|T|+1)! is exactly factorialShapleyInteraction. Substitute u and T⊆N. The finite convolution includes empty T, empty environments, and empty L, giving the source 1/(|S|+1) coefficient formula. The original Beta/zero-parameter/free-index errors remain visible; only the proof is repaired.',assumptions=['The classical factorial-weighted SII definition and a finite universe'])
put('theorem6','All branches of the Shapley–Taylor interaction formula','Use the source k/n times the reciprocal choose(n−1,|S|) weight, the same finite N and u, positive k, and T⊆N. The shared proof covers |T|<k, |T|=k, and |T|>k. At the critical order, positive-order factorial convolution gives reciprocal choose(|S|+k,k), including every empty-environment branch. Original Beta/zero-parameter/free-index errors stay visible; the original three-branch statement is unchanged.',assumptions=['The original STI definition has 1≤k≤n; all three branches are retained'])
put('noise-linearity','Linear interaction decomposition of masked-output noise','Center the noisy game and distribute its finite transform. Independence is unnecessary for this algebraic identity and is not inferred.',[r'''If g′(S)=g(S)+ε_S, then g′₀(S)=g₀(S)+(ε_S−ε_empty). Set e₀(S)=ε_S−ε_empty and distribute the finite sum:
$I_{g'_0}(S)=\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|T|}[g_0(T)+e_0(T)]=I_{g_0}(S)+I_{e_0}(S)$.
Both empty interactions vanish. For nonempty S the constant ε_empty has zero alternating coefficient, so the noise coefficient is also $\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|T|}\varepsilon_T$. This needs no independence and implies no variance amplification. The insufficient source variance premise remains separate. Use the compiled `interaction_add` and `interaction_centered_nonempty`.'''])
put('noise-variance','The claimed 2^|S| noise-variance amplification','The source’s “fully random noise” does not explicitly state independent equal variances; a completely correlated example defeats the unrestricted claim.',[r'''Take ε_T=Z for every T, with nondegenerate Z. Centering gives e₀=0, so the noise-interaction variance is zero, rather than 2^{|S|} times Var(Z). Adding IID would change the original unrestricted claim. In contrast, Appendix D of ICLR Generalizable explicitly gives IID Gaussian noise and fixed base interactions, so that paper’s corresponding theorem can use the same tools rigorously. Preserve the cross-paper premise difference.'''])
put('parity-mask','Parity-dependent signs in the masked-output example','The displayed odd/even example conflicts with the paper’s centered empty convention.',[r'''The source example gives u(S)=1 for odd |S| and −1 otherwise. At the empty set it is −1, contradicting the global u(empty)=0 definition. Preserve the example and its interaction-sign inference on official PDF9. Do not silently change its empty value or rename u as raw output and then claim completion. This definition conflict is distinct from average monotonicity in Assumption 2.'''])
put('or-density','Discussion of dense AND effects from a high-order OR','There is no quantitative formula or proof in this passage; its formal OR definition is linked to Appendix D.2.')
put('periodic-density','Illustration of nonsparse interactions for a sine output','No x_i domain or threshold is given. The passage does not prove that every sine-input example is nonsparse.')
put('parity-task','Empirical large-p observations for parity classification','The training accuracy and p interval are experimental results.')
put('transfer-inference','The transferability inference from sparsity and matching','A fixed additive model has same-class samples with disjoint salient singleton supports. Sparsity and exact matching alone do not imply transfer.',[r'''Take the same model $v(z)=\sum_{i=1}^n z_i$, input baseline zero, and samples x^(j)=e_j. Every output is 1, so classification by v(z)>1/2 puts all samples in the same positive class, satisfying the original same-class setting. Their centered masked games are g₀^(j)(S)=1 if j∈S and zero otherwise. Pure-singleton coefficient distribution gives I^(j)({j})=1 with all other coefficients zero. Each sample is maximally sparse and exactly reconstructs every mask, but different samples have disjoint salient families. The model needs only n singleton patterns, not the exponential explosion used in the source contradiction. Preserve this as a false inference, without adding a pattern-budget assumption to replace it.'''])
put('or-reverse','OR coefficients and reversed-mask AND coefficients','A reversed centered game supplies the exact sign and baseline convention.',[r'''For nonempty S the source defines $I_\lor(S)=-\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|T|}g_0(N\setminus T)$ and separately sets its empty value to zero. Define h(T)=g₀(N)−g₀(N\T); then h(empty)=0. For nonempty S, the alternating sum of the constant g₀(N) is zero, so $I_h(S)=g_0(N)\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|T|}-\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|T|}g_0(N\setminus T)=I_\lor(S)$. Both empty values are also zero. This preserves the negative sign in reversing masked/present states. Shared OR reconstruction is reused, but this paper’s centered empty zero differs from Generalizable’s raw output baseline convention.'''])
put('and-or-matching','Exact matching by the AND–OR decomposition','Apply complete AND and OR reconstruction to the same centered components, with both empty values zero.',[r'''The final source decomposition is g₀(S)=g_∧(S)+g_∨(S), with both components zero at empty. Apply the Möbius transform to g_∧ and the reversed OR definition to g_∨. This is externally cited and has no new detailed local author proof. The shared reconstructions require the same finite universe, one fixed decomposition for every mask, zero component empty values, and the separately defined zero OR empty coefficient. They give $g_\land(S)=\sum_{T\subseteq S}I_\land(T)$ and $g_\lor(S)=\sum_{T\cap S\ne\varnothing}I_\lor(T)$; add them to obtain Eq. (62). The component-name and γ-sign misprints in the parameter sentence are recorded at the algorithm-formula level and do not alter this final matching proposition.'''])
put('and-or-optimization','Sparse AND–OR and denoising optimization definitions','Preserve the source’s duplicated u_and name and the two +γ terms after denoising. An objective alone does not guarantee a globally sparsest computed solution.')
put('threshold-discussion','Discussion of exact zeros below the threshold','The author allows small nonzero interactions. No probability model or numerical theorem is supplied; this is not a strict proof.')
put('monotonicity-example','The five-variable binary mean-monotonicity example','Correct the one source arithmetic entry while keeping the same polynomial and monotonicity conclusion.',[
r'''The model is $v(z)=z_1z_2z_3+z_1z_2+z_2z_3+z_2+z_3$, with x=(1,1,1,1,1), r=0, and unused variables 4,5. The ten pair outputs in lexicographic order are 2,1,0,0,3,1,1,1,1,0, totaling 10 and averaging 1. The printed triple outputs are 5,2,2,1,1,0,3,3,1,0. The final mask {3,4,5} retains z₃=1, so its output is 1. The corrected triple list ends in 1, totals 19, and averages 1.9 rather than 1.8. The same source monotonicity statement is still 1≤1.9. Preserve the original table and error location separately.''',
r'''Count the polynomial’s two first-order, two second-order, and one third-order monomials. For 0≤m≤5, $\bar g_0^{(m)}=2m/5+2\binom m2/\binom52+\binom m3/\binom53$. The full mean sequence is 0,0.4,1,1.9,3.2,5, nondecreasing. This verifies the original example, not a general monotonicity theorem for all DNNs.'''])
put('sampling-complexity','Sampling layer means with O(nt) model queries','Count the requested model evaluations, and separate query count from runtime and concentration.',[
r'''For each m the estimator uses t sampled m-element sets and is $t^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^t g_0(S_j)$. Positive layers m=1,…,n request exactly nt evaluations. If t queries are also made at m=0, there are (n+1)t requests, bounded by 2nt when n≥1. Thus model-query cost is O(nt). Full enumeration has 2^n masks and O(2^n) requests.''',
r'''Inference time per DNN call, subset sampling, and estimator computation have separate runtime costs. The source supplies no error bound or confidence level and does not prove that finite sampling strictly certifies all mean-monotonicity conditions or Assumption 3. Approximate testing is not relabeled as deterministic verification.'''])
put('experiments','Empirical sparsity, mean monotonicity, and p values','Classify all experimental tables, plots, and parameters as empirical content rather than proofs.')
put('related-work','Externally cited mathematical and empirical results','This is background. Each cited paper’s proof is not automatically imported into this paper’s target count.')
step_titles={
'derivative-cutoff':['Classical mixed partials and their existence order','Commutation of finite differences and true partial derivatives','Zero mixed partials imply zero rectangular differences','Bridge from rectangular differences to actual coordinate masks','Apply the original high-order multiindex condition','Recover the original centered interaction and scope','A two-variable example with a nonzero output baseline'],
'reconstruction':['Definitions and original target','Empty set and finite reindexing','The surviving term and output baseline'],
'lemma2':['Definitions and original target','Reconstruction and grouping by order','Bijection for masks containing a fixed interaction','The binomial ratio','Centering and order cutoff','Boundary and three-variable check'],
'lemma3':['Definitions and original target','Repair the determinant sign','Complete finite-difference induction'],
 'theorem2':['Definitions and original target','Bounds from the original mean assumptions','Positive-mean normalization and logarithmic bound','Zero means force every order total to vanish','Witnesses when the first mean is zero','Low-digit indices and all source constraints'],
'asymptotic-claim':['Scope of the asymptotic interpretation','Infinite parameter family and sign partition','Actual model and all interaction coefficients','Check the original three assumptions','Threshold, cancellation ratio, and same-order density','Exact Theorem 3 and the scope of the refuted inference'],
 'theorem3':['Definitions and original target','Threshold counting and cancellation','Substitute the same witnesses and retain Case 1'],
'monotonicity-example':['Original example and arithmetic repair','Layer means for this example only'],
'sampling-complexity':['Layer-mean sampling and model queries','Query count versus runtime and certification']}
common_justification='The original premises, explicit finite-index arguments, and complete shared proofs are retained. Repairs change proof steps only.'
out={}
for r in src['results']:
 t=E[r['id']];k=r['id'].removeprefix('iclr2024-sparse-');steps=t['steps']
 if not steps and r['proof_steps']:steps=[r'The complete shared proof is available through its stable shared ID; the following is the paper-specific premise adapter.\n\n'+t['overview']]
 assert len(steps)==len(r['proof_steps']),(k,len(steps),len(r['proof_steps']))
 z={a:t[a] for a in ('title','overview')};z['assumptions']=t['assumptions'] if t['assumptions'] is not None else copy.deepcopy(r['assumptions']);z['definitions']=copy.deepcopy(r['definitions']);z['completion_scope']=r.get('completion_scope','Exact source scope as separately classified');z['proof_scope']=r.get('proof_scope',z['completion_scope']);z['proof_steps']=[]
 for i,(s,b) in enumerate(zip(r['proof_steps'],steps)):
  refs=copy.deepcopy(s.get('lean_refs',[]))
  for ref in refs:
   if isinstance(ref,dict) and 'explanation_md' in ref:ref['explanation_md']='This real declaration covers the exact scope stated in the verification report; an association does not independently verify every explanatory sentence.'
  z['proof_steps'].append(dict(id=s['id'],title=step_titles.get(k,['Definitions, original target, and premise adapter']*len(steps))[i],body_md=b.replace('\\n\\n','\n\n'),formula_tex=s.get('formula_tex',''),justification=common_justification if s.get('justification') else '',lean_refs=refs))
 z['original_statement_note']='The official source statement is preserved. Project translations and mathematical transcriptions are distinguished from the PDF layout.'
 z['original_proof_note']='The complete original mathematical proof remains linked to the official source. Its errors are explicitly recorded; the project rewriting does not silently change the proposition.'
 z['notation_map']=copy.deepcopy(r.get('notation_map',[]))
 for n in z['notation_map']:
  if 'note' in n:n['note']='The definition and source scope determine this notation mapping; centering and empty-set conventions are retained.'
 out[r['id']]=z
